[{"short_id":"fonp7g","created_at":"2026-10-19T17:53:54.341-05:00","title":"Intermediary between public and private members","url":"","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eSay I were to have a\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003estruct Foo { pub public: Bar, restricted: Qux }\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ewhere ‘restricted’ is some member variable which I want to be able to get, but not set, from outside of the struct methods. Normally, I would write a getter method\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eimpl Foo { pub fn restricted(\u0026amp;self) -\u0026gt; \u0026amp;Qux { self.restricted }}\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ewhich isn’t very much typing, and probably I could generate these automatically with an IDE or a plugin.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBut all the same, why not build this into the declaration syntax with a modifier in between \u003ccode\u003eprivate\u003c/code\u003e (implicit in Rust but some languages have it) and \u003ccode\u003epublic\u003c/code\u003e? Maybe something like \u003ccode\u003eread\u003c/code\u003e so that we could have\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003estruct Foo { x, read y, pub z }\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhere x is genuinely private to the struct and can only be read or written by member functions, y can be read by anyone but not modified arbitrarily, and z can be read or written to by anyone?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis would need to distinguish between access and mutation of foo.y, which may not be trivial but doesn’t seem outwardly especially difficult.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m wondering if there are hidden gotchas here that make this a lot more complex from a language design perspective, or less ergonomic than I’m assuming. If not, it seems like a straightforward feature for future languages to implement, worth an extra keyword, I would think.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd if it it possible and desirable, what should the default be? It makes some sense to be that read-only is a more sensible default than full privacy if all three states are available.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEager to hear people’s thoughts.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Say I were to have a \r\n\r\n`struct Foo { pub public: Bar, restricted: Qux }`\r\n\r\nwhere ‘restricted’ is some member variable which I want to be able to get, but not set, from outside of the struct methods. Normally, I would write a getter method\r\n\r\n`impl Foo { pub fn restricted(\u0026self) -\u003e \u0026Qux { self.restricted }}`\r\n\r\nwhich isn’t very much typing, and probably I could generate these automatically with an IDE or a plugin.\r\n\r\nBut all the same, why not build this into the declaration syntax with a modifier in between `private` (implicit in Rust but some languages have it) and `public`? Maybe something like `read` so that we could have \r\n\r\n`struct Foo { x, read y, pub z }`\r\n\r\nWhere x is genuinely private to the struct and can only be read or written by member functions, y can be read by anyone but not modified arbitrarily, and z can be read or written to by anyone?\r\n\r\nThis would need to distinguish between access and mutation of foo.y, which may not be trivial but doesn’t seem outwardly especially difficult.\r\n\r\nI’m wondering if there are hidden gotchas here that make this a lot more complex from a language design perspective, or less ergonomic than I’m assuming. If not, it seems like a straightforward feature for future languages to implement, worth an extra keyword, I would think.\r\n\r\nAnd if it it possible and desirable, what should the default be? It makes some sense to be that read-only is a more sensible default than full privacy if all three states are available.\r\n\r\nEager to hear people’s thoughts.","submitter_user":"nrposner","user_is_author":true,"tags":["plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fonp7g","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fonp7g/intermediary_between_public_private"},{"short_id":"tsyxpn","created_at":"2026-10-19T17:31:42.404-05:00","title":"Blocking Identity Resolution Infrastructure","url":"https://webkit.org/blog/18438/blocking-identity-resolution-infrastructure/","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ahobson","user_is_author":false,"tags":["privacy","web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/tsyxpn","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/tsyxpn/blocking_identity_resolution"},{"short_id":"nne5uh","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:10:46.398-05:00","title":"The Netherlands is building its own Linux OS to break free from Microsoft","url":"https://www.techspot.com/news/114018-netherlands-building-own-linux-os-break-free-microsoft.html","score":6,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"pocket_walrus","user_is_author":false,"tags":["linux","windows"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nne5uh","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nne5uh/netherlands_is_building_its_own_linux_os"},{"short_id":"b36ixw","created_at":"2026-10-19T13:25:48.327-05:00","title":"In Context","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5eO7BNyJao","score":12,"flags":0,"comment_count":6,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"veqq","user_is_author":false,"tags":["programming","video"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/b36ixw","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/b36ixw/context"},{"short_id":"ncwfdh","created_at":"2026-10-19T13:15:04.880-05:00","title":"Demystifying Spherical Harmonics: An Optimization Approach","url":"https://andrew-helmer.github.io/demystifying-spherical-harmonics/","score":1,"flags":1,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ocharles","user_is_author":false,"tags":["graphics"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ncwfdh","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ncwfdh/demystifying_spherical_harmonics"},{"short_id":"c2ouz0","created_at":"2026-10-19T12:52:41.834-05:00","title":"2026 is an exciting y